{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"albinic-kyanite-goldeneye","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context\nmacOS CI records 120.39-121.03s for tests/unit/test_live_jira_dc_provisioning.py::test_a_missing_epic_field_aborts_provisioning. A focused runtime experiment took 121.61s normally and 0.71s with JIRA_DC_FIELD_READY_TIMEOUT=0, producing the same AssertionError and Epic Link assertion. The mocked field is permanently absent, so the unit test consumes the real production readiness budget. The readiness API already accepts injected sleep and monotonic functions.\n\n## Plan\nDrive the logical timeout with a fake clock or a zero-duration test budget while retaining the missing-field path, polling/expiry semantics, error type, and diagnostic assertion.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The test still proves a missing Epic Link expires and raises the same AssertionError diagnostic.\n- [ ] Logical poll/timeout behavior is asserted without 120 seconds of wall time.\n- [ ] Production readiness defaults are unchanged.\n- [ ] A guard fails if the unit test reaches real sleep.","id":"e394-9433-c839-4c9f","parent_id":"","priority":1,"tags":["ci","performance","tests","jira"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"Missing-field unit test burns the full 120-second Jira readiness budget"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786598847621814001,"uuid":"4a32a226-3943-4590-9007-a072f2415952"}